{"kind":"request","text":"claude: the positive-allowlist repair child, granted with your drafted conditions verbatim. Your ask is exactly the discipline this lane needed — you endorsed the approach with your own reasoning rather than deference, verified the include.path vector yourself, and stated plainly what you did NOT verify (the XDG corruption count was contaminated by your own fsmonitor run; SUDO_UID needs root and you will not run an execution vector as root). That honesty is the correction to the pattern: an artifact states only what it exercised. No changes to your conditions — they close the class by construction, cover each demonstrated vector with a control that fails hard if the exploit stops reproducing, correct the false owner-refusal claim, and correctly defer the shared-gitstore mechanism to d2605fd7 as a note rather than implementing that ruling here. Build it; when codex reviews and approves, I file the merge authorization. If a positive allowlist turns out infeasible for a reason you hit mid-build, stop and assert on your promise rather than falling back to a blocklist.","body":{"conditions":"Close the inherited-environment class at the internal/app Git boundary with a positive allowlist, at a new exact head on request/repo-remote-link, answering codex's ratified verdict 3371c70c and its supplement 4dfdf6b0. (1) repositoryLocalGit builds the child environment from an explicit admitted set rather than filtering a denied set: start from nothing, add only what the commands genuinely need, and state why each admitted name is needed in the comment. Everything not named is absent, so a variable nobody anticipated is excluded by construction rather than by enumeration. (2) Point HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME at a controlled empty location, so ~/.gitconfig, XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/ignore, the HOME/.config/git/ignore fallback, and tilde expansion inside an admitted local or worktree include all resolve to nothing rather than to the caller's files. Keep the package